WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.670
- The Good Doctor is being
recommended more than

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.790
Grey's Anatomy by you guys
in my comment section.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.930
And in fact, this episode has popped up

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:09.300
more times than any other.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.260
So, let's get started.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:11.128
Be-woop!

00:00:11.128 --> 00:00:13.795
(upbeat music)

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:17.968
- I think you may need a new mentor.

00:00:17.968 --> 00:00:21.390
(melancholy music)

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:22.440
I'm dying.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:23.500
- Ooh.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:24.370
That's a concern.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:26.660
I know I only watched the first
two episodes with you guys,

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.180
and now I'm on the 18th and it feels like

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.840
I'm skipping around like crazy,

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.420
but this was voted the number
one episode by Episode Ninja,

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.080
so I feel like I had to check it out.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.300
Don't judge me.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:37.133
I'm excited.

00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:39.190
- 12 to 18 months.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.400
And I'd appreciate it if
you didn't tell anyone.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.630
- I wanna know the diagnosis.
- Okay, I will in my own time.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:45.733
- Okay, have you had a full workup?

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:47.690
- Yes, of course.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:50.830
- It could be a secondary
tumor metastasized

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:52.290
to your brain from somewhere else.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.470
- It could be a primary tumor,

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:55.940
which means that it
originated in the brain.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.910
Or it could be a secondary tumor,

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.700
let's say a lung tumor
that started in the lungs,

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.600
through the lymphatic system or the blood

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.300
went into the brain and then
created a secondary tumor.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.770
- You should get a second opinion.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.800
- Shaun, I've been a
neurosurgeon for 30 years.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.639
I think that qualifies
as a second opinion.

00:01:11.639 --> 00:01:14.470
- (laughs) You know what's
interesting about being a doctor

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:17.480
who's practiced 30-something
years as a neurosurgeon?

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.390
You actually make really bad decisions

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:20.750
when it comes to your own health.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:23.380
It's notorious that doctors
are really bad patients,

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:24.800
and I totally agree with this.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:25.750
I'm the worst.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:27.370
And in fact, if I have a patient that

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:28.730
I'm treating that I know is a doctor,

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:30.720
I know I'm gonna have to take extra time.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.350
I know I'm gonna have
to do a little arguing

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:33.300
with them in order to get them

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.840
to take care of themselves properly.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.610
- Well, given the degree of the rotation

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.820
and the fact that the
break is almost compound,

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.352
I say we're dealing with a bimalleolar,

00:01:40.352 --> 00:01:41.747
not a trimalleolar fracture.

00:01:41.747 --> 00:01:42.620
- Very good.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:45.571
What else?
- Dr. Darr to radiology.

00:01:45.571 --> 00:01:47.160
Darr to radiology.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:47.993
- Color?

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.020
- Blood flow's restricted.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.066
His foot's dying.

00:01:52.066 --> 00:01:53.300
- Oh no.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.470
- So, they're talking
about this ankle fracture.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:56.900
And when they say it's
a compound fracture,

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.010
that means it's fractured
in multiple places.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.190
When there's lack of blood flow,

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:01.650
that means there's pressure being put

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.720
on the arteries or veins in that area,

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.810
which is very dangerous
'cause that's called

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.160
a vascular compromise,
meaning not enough blood

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.290
is going to that area and that
area is necrosing or dying.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:12.730
- I'm gonna have to reset it now.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.610
Hold his leg.
- When they say resetting,

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.106
it's exactly what you think it is.

00:02:18.106 --> 00:02:18.941
Gah!

00:02:18.941 --> 00:02:19.774
- It's just gonna take a second.

00:02:19.774 --> 00:02:21.580
I need you to count to three.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:22.413
One.

00:02:22.413 --> 00:02:23.799
- One, two.

00:02:23.799 --> 00:02:25.410
(bone clicks)
Ah! (grunts)

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:26.740
- I think that was kinda accurate.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.218
I don't know, even my scream matched it.

00:02:29.218 --> 00:02:32.120
(heart monitor beeping)

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.660
- Shouldn't I be asleep for this?

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:35.980
- [Neil] Well, your blood alcohol level

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.510
complicates anesthesia, and we
think you have a concussion,

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.800
so we wanna keep you responsive.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:43.390
We did give you a regional nerve block,

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:44.980
so you shouldn't feel anything.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:46.450
- This is actually similar to what

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.960
we do when we do a C-section.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.940
The mother is not asleep.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.670
We do a regional block just like this.

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.010
We need the mother conscious in case

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.540
we need to make decisions
so that she can help

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:58.670
facilitate the process
if anything happens.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:02.490
- I had a friend, a
neurosurgeon at SF Muni.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:03.690
She looked at the scan.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.140
She came to the same diagnosis.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:09.160
Shaun, I have an inoperable glioma.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.020
- Glioma is a type of tumor.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:12.430
When we say a tumor is inoperable,

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:15.720
usually that's because
the area where it's found.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.590
How encompassing is it?

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:18.840
If a tumor's spread throughout the

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.410
entire brain, how are you gonna operate?

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:21.940
You can't take out the entire brain.

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.330
Sometimes if we're very
lucky and it's localized

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:27.240
to a specific region, we
can just take that part out.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.940
But even then, we're at risk at harming

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.230
the surrounding structures of the brain

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.650
and leaving someone with
neurological defects.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:34.880
And you can imagine how
awful that could be.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.640
Yeah, okay, take out
my tumor, help me live,

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.570
but then I can no longer speak.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.180
Horrible, horrible situations.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:41.013
- Be reasonable.

00:03:41.013 --> 00:03:42.540
- Beg your pardon?

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.170
- Be reasonable.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:45.923
- I have no idea what
you're talking about.

00:03:45.923 --> 00:03:47.690
- Well, it'll be clear.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:48.550
What the hell happened to Caden?

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:50.063
- I don't know, he twisted his ankle, I--

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:51.005
- You've lied to me before.

00:03:51.005 --> 00:03:52.810
You're lying to me now,
you spoiled little coward.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:54.330
- Hey, hey.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:55.163
Be reasonable.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.080
His friend's hurt, he's scared,
and you're freaking him out.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.130
- Good cop, bad cop.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:02.090
The reason good cop, bad cop works so well

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:05.230
is exactly based on the principle
that I've told you before

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:07.250
in some of my Wednesday Checkup videos.

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:09.850
So, if you want someone
to see your point of view,

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.180
you wanna influence
someone in a positive way,

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.190
it's not ideal to put
a stone in between you.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:16.150
It's not ideal to just
state your argument,

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:17.760
then be confrontational about it

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.830
'cause that person won't
wanna change their beliefs.

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:22.380
But now on the other hand,
if you can get that person

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.350
to see that you're on their side,

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:26.270
that you have the same intentions,

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:29.350
you're also a human, and they
can see eye to eye with you,

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:31.300
you're more likely to
convince them of something.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.100
And good cop, bad cop is a fast

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:34.430
track to achieving that goal.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.250
- We were pledging, and there's this

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:39.933
wheel of torture thing you
gotta do for initiation.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.300
- Torture.
- But it's mostly

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.180
stupid stuff, dude.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.520
It's like they drink a cup of olive oil

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.620
or eat a tablespoon of cinnamon.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:48.453
- Or jump off a roof.

00:04:48.453 --> 00:04:49.640
- And what did Caden land on?

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:50.860
- Laundry.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:52.060
- He had to do laundry?

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:56.080
- No, like he had to eat
those laundry things.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:56.913
- Oh my god, Tide POD--

00:04:56.913 --> 00:04:57.920
- As many as he could--
- Challenge?

00:04:57.920 --> 00:04:59.030
- In 30 seconds.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:04:59.920
- Detergent.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:01.261
Eat detergent.

00:05:01.261 --> 00:05:02.653
And how many did he have?

00:05:02.653 --> 00:05:05.210
- I don't know, like six, maybe seven.

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:06.670
- I feel like I shouldn't be here,

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:09.080
even on a medical channel,
explaining to you why that's bad.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.160
I mean, it's like, don't eat knives,

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.640
don't eat forks, and don't eat Tide PODS.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.160
The only time that I'll say that

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:17.440
someone may accidentally
ingest a Tide POD is

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.010
if you have a child, very young,

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:21.890
who's not aware, and
they see a colorful pod

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:23.380
and they think it's candy
and they swallow it.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.680
But if you're an adult or an adolescent

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.030
and you're eating Tide PODS, come on.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:28.730
You can do better, I know you can.

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:31.403
That's my spiel.

00:05:31.403 --> 00:05:33.630
- The trauma SOD's asking
for you in OR number two.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.690
We got an emergency thoracotomy.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:37.200
- [Neil] Who can give me the
remaining steps for closing?

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.080
- [Morgan] Cut anchoring sutures,

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:39.540
clear laps, and reposition the bowel.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.360
Remove clamps from the
duodenal resection site,

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:43.070
keeping clear of the underlying aorta.

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:45.440
- When he says clearing
laps, what he's talking about

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.190
are those pads that we use
to control the bleeding

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:51.290
or to put pressure or to clean the site

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:52.870
so that we can actually
see what we're doing

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:54.310
when a patient has some blood in place.

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:55.730
The nurses that are there with us,

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:57.010
the assistants that are there with us

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:59.750
are counting each one of those laps.

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:03.600
Because if we leave one of
those in, horrible situation.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.830
It'll fester, bacterial
infection, all this stuff.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.340
So yes, we count every tool.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:10.770
We make sure everything
is in, everything is out.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:14.240
We even have a little wall of pockets,

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.300
almost like a shoe organizer
where we put each one

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.070
of the things, and then we
count them out afterwards.

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:20.442
- [Shaun] While you run the bowel for me.

00:06:20.442 --> 00:06:23.192
(stirring music)

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:27.710
- When we say run the
bowel, what we're doing is

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:29.430
we're looking for any
injuries to the bowel.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.370
Most commonly this happens
in a gunshot wound.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:33.410
If someone gets shot in the abdomen,

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:36.890
right away the first step
in the trauma situation

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:39.600
is to open the patient, do
an explorative laparotomy,

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:41.240
which means that you cut the stomach open,

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:43.940
you run the bowel, you see if
there's any injury anywhere.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:45.850
'Cause if there's a lot of
blood pooling in the abdomen,

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:47.650
the patient could die very quickly,

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:49.850
and you have the run the
bowel quickly but effectively

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:52.009
to make sure that you find
the area that's bleeding.

00:06:52.009 --> 00:06:54.769
(dramatic music)

00:06:54.769 --> 00:06:56.186
- [Claire] Shaun?

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:59.150
- [Morgan] Shaun, is everything all right?

00:06:59.150 --> 00:06:59.983
- [Neil] What happened this time?

00:06:59.983 --> 00:07:01.730
- He's got a bleed somewhere.

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:02.940
- I want a capsule endoscopy to check

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:04.860
the entire length of the
bowel for bleeding and leaks.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:07.160
And give me a complete
coagulation and metabolic profile.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.010
- A capsule endoscopy
is when you take a pill

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:10.400
that actually has a camera in it,

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:12.820
and it takes about 24 hours
to go through the system,

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:16.990
and you get quality photos
consistently, photos and video.

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:19.450
It records its way through the GI system,

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:21.460
which allows you to see if
there's any bleeding anywhere.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:24.140
In my past experience,
what I've seen is that

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.950
if someone is bleeding and we suspect

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:29.260
it's from an upper GI source,
meaning from the stomach,

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:31.790
the esophagus, what we
do is we do an endoscopy

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:33.310
where you put the camera in the mouth

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:34.930
and you try and find where
that bleed is coming from,

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:37.280
and you can control that
bleed in a number of ways.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:38.680
If you think it's from a lower GI bleed,

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:40.780
you could do a colonoscopy,
which is just another form

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:43.130
of endoscopy where you
put a camera up the butt.

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:45.320
Not sure if they're doing
a capsule endoscopy here

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:48.370
just to look cool for TV
or because they don't feel

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:50.530
comfortable taking him for an endoscopy

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:52.200
because he's in a critical state.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.410
Now, he's getting lab work.

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:56.530
That is legitimate because
when you take detergent,

00:07:56.530 --> 00:07:59.130
it can mess up the acid
base balance in your blood.

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:00.970
It can mess with the
electrolytes of your blood.

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:04.230
And if it messes with them
too much, it can kill you.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:06.630
- Our patient has
disseminated intravascular

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:07.610
coagulation.
- Ooh.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:09.860
- [Neil] Can anyone tell
me why that is very bad?

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:11.790
- Blood clots form
throughout the blood vessels

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:13.770
and rapidly deplete the
body's clotting factors,

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:15.700
thereby causing a systematic bleed.

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:17.210
- It's also a catch-22.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:18.460
If we treat the clotting,

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:19.950
he might end up with a terminal bleed.

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:23.740
- So, DIC is actually
a very rare condition.

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:24.970
It's life-threatening.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:28.000
The last time I saw it was
probably in my residency

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:29.830
when I was at the intensive care unit.

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:32.870
And it's a condition where
you have excessive clotting

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:34.660
going on within the blood vessels.

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:36.070
So it's intravascular.

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:38.310
And when that happens,
you can have organs die,

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:41.640
think stroke, pulmonary
embolism in the lungs.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:44.510
Just think of it simply
put as a clotting condition

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:45.890
where your body's clotting a lot,

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.140
and because it's clotting a lot,

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:49.800
it's using up all of its
clotting factors and platelets,

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.030
which then makes you more
susceptible to bleed.

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.150
- He could have a crush
injury from his ankle.

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:54.320
- Or a bacterial infection he

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:55.890
picked up before or during surgery.

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:57.450
- It might've been set off by drug use.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:59.364
- Or we did this to him.

00:08:59.364 --> 00:09:02.370
(stirring music)

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:03.873
And by we, I mean Shaun.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:06.230
He spaced out in the OR when

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:08.660
he was closing, kind of like he is now.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:09.840
- I'm listening.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:10.810
- Comforting.

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:12.080
He wasn't focused.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:13.690
- He got some bad news.

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:14.960
- About what?

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:16.210
- It's a personal matter.

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:21.033
- Shaun was distracted
by a personal issue?

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:24.303
What, did Lea come back?

00:09:26.191 --> 00:09:28.300
Does she have a new boyfriend?

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:29.923
His cable get cut off?

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.123
- I have an inoperable glioma.

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:36.280
18 months.

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:39.010
- There were some
surprises on your images.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:41.000
The previous diagnosis was incorrect.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:42.910
You have a glioblastoma

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:44.280
multiforme located--
- Which is worse.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:45.219
- In your pons.

00:09:45.219 --> 00:09:46.240
- Oh!

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:47.680
- [Oncologist] I'm sure I
don't need to tell you this,

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:51.379
but it's the most aggressive
form of brain cancer.

00:09:51.379 --> 00:09:52.680
- Ugh!

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:57.330
- [Oncologist] We estimate
three, maybe four months.

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:59.084
(phone dings and vibrates)

00:09:59.084 --> 00:10:00.555
- Uh oh, someone's in trouble.

00:10:00.555 --> 00:10:03.114
(cheerful merry-go-round music)

00:10:03.114 --> 00:10:04.756
- Do you need me to listen to you anymore?

00:10:04.756 --> 00:10:08.794
- No, Shaun, it's okay.
(Mike laughs)

00:10:08.794 --> 00:10:10.023
- Do you need me to listen!

00:10:10.023 --> 00:10:13.720
- Hope is irrelevant for me.

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:15.772
Hope is painful.

00:10:15.772 --> 00:10:17.670
(somber music)

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.840
I don't wanna spend what's left

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:23.180
of my life chasing my
tail around in a circle.

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:25.710
- See, I disagree with him a
little bit on the hope aspect.

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:26.960
I understand what he's feeling,

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:28.740
and it's part of the process.

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:31.590
But hope is really important
in the medical community.

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:33.740
Hope is very important
when you have a disease.

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:35.970
Because when you have
hope, you can have a more

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:38.710
positive mental state, and
a more positive mental state

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:40.000
will give you the best chances,

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:41.980
irrespective of what illness you have.

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:44.160
If you have a cold,
you'll feel better sooner

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:45.880
if your mental state is better.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:47.610
If you have pain, you'll feel less of it

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:48.960
if your mental state is better.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:52.690
So I think maintaining hope
is one of those ways nature

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:54.910
allows us to have a natural relief

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:56.770
of whatever suffering we're going through.

00:10:56.770 --> 00:10:58.440
So I like to hold out hope.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:01.786
- Your tumor is located
at the base of your skull.

00:11:01.786 --> 00:11:03.340
(stirring music)

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:08.340
An open cranial biopsy
would tell us what's wrong.

00:11:08.656 --> 00:11:10.500
But there are blood vessels in the way.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:13.803
The biopsy would cause
an aneurysm or a bleed.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:18.830
But if we go through your
nasal cribriform plate,

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:21.990
we can cross the tentorium
on the contralateral side

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:24.063
and avoid all those blood vessels.

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:30.430
We can do a biopsy through your nose.

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:33.010
- Caden is still alive, and
if we can keep it that way,

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:35.340
there won't be an M and M review,
which means no one will be

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:37.760
asking questions and no one
will have to give answers.

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:41.210
- M and M is a morbidity
and mortality conference.

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:43.350
That happens if someone
gets really sick or dies.

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:45.590
We take some of the most complex cases

00:11:45.590 --> 00:11:48.160
during these meetings, and you
have all the residents there,

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:50.390
all the doctors that
participated in the case,

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:52.900
some specialists are
required to attend this.

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:56.370
And basically we dissect the
case from the beginning to end

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:57.910
trying to figure out
what we can do better,

00:11:57.910 --> 00:12:01.410
learn from our mistakes, use
it as a learning opportunity.

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:04.540
And I've seen these be
incredibly educational,

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:06.070
but I've also seen them backfire,

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:08.660
see doctors get into near fistfights.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:10.456
- Is that from an IV?

00:12:10.456 --> 00:12:12.710
(hopeful music)

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:14.453
You did the biopsy.

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:16.443
- I did.

00:12:18.714 --> 00:12:21.155
I have a low-grade glioma.

00:12:21.155 --> 00:12:22.930
- Not a GBM?

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:24.600
I told you.

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:26.350
- Cancer, Shaun, it's still cancer.

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:29.140
I have to undergo brain surgery,

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:31.765
which is really scary because
I'm not the one performing it.

00:12:31.765 --> 00:12:32.880
(Mike laughs)
Five days a week

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:37.580
for six weeks of radiation,
10 more weeks of chemotherapy.

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:38.497
- And then?

00:12:42.673 --> 00:12:45.173
- And then with a little luck,

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:49.402
you and I can go to the
Super Bowl next season.

00:12:49.402 --> 00:12:50.433
- Yay!

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:57.560
I almost became a concierge doctor.

00:12:57.560 --> 00:12:58.840
If you wanna know what that is,

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:01.940
check out my medical drama
review of Royal Pains.

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:02.820
Click here.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:05.470
Click it, click it, click it, click it!

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:06.741
Stay happy and healthy.

00:13:06.741 --> 00:13:07.872
(upbeat music)

